Scene & Lore
The movement flared to life across London between 1967 and 1969 during the chaotic construction of the Westway elevated motorway. Classical conservatory runaways, squatter syndicates, and grease-stained blues purists—known colloquially as "The Gantrymen"—pried open cast-iron junction chassis with crowbars, hot-wiring their 100-watt valve amplifiers into the high-voltage conduits powering municipal traffic lights. Dressed in oilskin mackintoshes draped over frayed Edwardian velvet coats, they staged unsanctioned jams atop pedestrian flyovers, weaponizing red-and-amber phases to physically paralyze metropolitan transit while massive brass sections and shrieking lead guitars echoed through the gridlock. Their concerts were volatile civil-disobedience rallies: crowd lookouts tapped Morse-code rhythms on lampposts to cue the orchestra, and performances ended only when police utility crews physically severed the street-level cables.

